  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Real-Axis Projection of a Phasor",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the cosine-valued time signal obtained by projecting a rotating phasor onto the real axis using its magnitude, angular frequency, rotation direction, and initial phase.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Real-Axis Projection of a Phasor",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Real-Axis Projection of a Phasor",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "image-needed",
        "image_slots": [
          {
            "image_slot_id": "section-001",
            "placement": "section",
            "location_placeholder": "[IMAGE-LOCATION:section-001]",
            "description_placeholder": "[IMAGE-DESCRIPTION:section-001]",
            "image_description": "A clean complex-plane diagram with horizontal real axis and vertical imaginary axis, a phasor vector of magnitude A from the origin at angle $\\theta$(t) counterclockwise from the positive real axis, a dashed horizontal projection to the real axis labeled A cos $\\theta$(t), and a small rotation arrow around the origin."
          }
        ],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A phasor is a vector in the complex plane whose tip moves as it rotates. Its real-axis projection is the horizontal shadow on the real axis, and that shadow is the cosine-valued signal we want to read from the motion.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "image",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": "section-001"
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the simplest base case: a unit phasor on the positive real axis projects to $1$. If you scale that same picture to magnitude $A$ and tilt it to angle $\\theta$, the real-axis shadow is the adjacent side, so its length is $A\\cos\\theta$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{Re}\\{A e^{j\\theta}\\}=A\\cos\\theta",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same rule is written compactly by taking the real part of $A e^{j\\theta}$. That notation says to keep the horizontal component of the phasor and ignore the vertical one.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now let the angle vary with time. For counterclockwise rotation at angular frequency $\\omega$ with initial phase $\\phi$, the angle is $\\theta(t)=\\omega t+\\phi$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t)=\\omega t+\\phi,\\qquad x(t)=\\operatorname{Re}\\{A e^{j(\\omega t+\\phi)}\\}=A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the real-axis projection of the rotating phasor is exactly the cosine signal $x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$. Later sections keep the same projection rule and vary only the rotation direction or the time at which you evaluate it.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Real-Axis Projection of a Phasor",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Projecting a Fixed Phasor onto the Real Axis",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor of magnitude $A$ sits at a fixed angle $\\phi$ above the positive real axis. What is its real-axis projection?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is a fixed phasor: its tip is not rotating yet, so we only read its current angle $\\phi$ and its magnitude $A$. The real-axis projection is the horizontal component of that vector.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For any phasor at angle $\\phi$ from the positive real axis, the horizontal component is the adjacent side of the right triangle. That means the projection is the magnitude times $\\cos\\phi$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{Re}\\{A e^{j\\phi}\\}=A\\cos\\phi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the real-axis projection is $A\\cos\\phi$. In complex-number language, this is just the real part of the phasor $A e^{j\\phi}$, which is the same rule the next steps will reuse when the angle begins to depend on time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Real-Axis Projection of a Phasor",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Writing the Real-Axis Projection of a Counterclockwise Rotating Phasor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor of magnitude $A$ starts at phase $\\phi$ and rotates counterclockwise with angular frequency $\\omega$. Write the real-axis projection signal $x(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is counterclockwise rotation, so the phasor angle is not fixed. It starts at $\\phi$ and increases linearly with time at rate $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t)=\\omega t+\\phi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same real-axis projection rule from the fixed-phasor case: keep the magnitude $A$ and take the cosine of the angle. That gives the horizontal shadow of the rotating phasor at time $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\operatorname{Re}\\{A e^{j(\\omega t+\\phi)}\\}=A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the real-axis projection signal is $x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$. The only new wrinkle is that the angle now depends on time, while the projection rule itself stays the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Real-Axis Projection of a Phasor",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing the Real-Axis Projection of a Clockwise Rotating Phasor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor of magnitude $A$ starts at phase $\\phi$ and rotates clockwise with angular frequency $\\omega$. Write the real-axis projection signal $x(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is clockwise rotation, so the phasor angle decreases from its starting value $\\phi$ as time increases. With angular frequency $\\omega$, the angle is $\\theta(t)=\\phi-\\omega t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t)=\\phi-\\omega t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same real-axis projection rule as in the previous example: keep the magnitude $A$ and take the cosine of the angle. The only thing that changes is the sign in the angle progression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\operatorname{Re}\\{A e^{j(\\phi-\\omega t)}\\}=A\\cos(\\phi-\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the real-axis projection signal is $x(t)=A\\cos(\\phi-\\omega t)$. The only new wrinkle is the direction of rotation, which makes the angle fall instead of rise.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Real-Axis Projection of a Phasor",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Computing Real-Axis Projection Values at Specific Times",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor of magnitude $A$ starts at phase $\\phi$ and rotates counterclockwise with angular frequency $\\omega$. What is its real-axis projection at time $t=t_0$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a specific time. For counterclockwise rotation, the phasor angle grows from its starting phase, so write the angle first as $\\theta(t)=\\omega t+\\phi$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t)=\\omega t+\\phi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the requested time before evaluating the cosine: $\\theta(t_0)=\\omega t_0+\\phi$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t_0)=\\omega t_0+\\phi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The real-axis projection keeps the magnitude $A$ and takes the cosine of the angle at that time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t_0)=A\\cos(\\theta(t_0))=A\\cos(\\omega t_0+\\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the exact projection value is $A\\cos(\\omega t_0+\\phi)$. If later data make the angle a special trig angle, simplify it from there.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
